          "rules": [
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A broad exception handler can hide failures it cannot recover from."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Broad exception handler** (`PY-COR-002`)\n\nA broad exception handler can hide failures it cannot recover from.\n\n**Remediation:** Catch the narrow exception types the operation can recover from.\n\n**Not reported when:**\n\n- The handler catches a specific exception class rather than Exception or BaseException.\n- The handler body is only `pass`/`...` (a bare `except:`, `except Exception: pass`, ...): PY-COR-003 reports the swallow and one handler yields one finding, not two.\n- The handler ends with a bare `raise` or `raise <bound name>` (no `from`): `except BaseException: cleanup(); raise` is a `finally` with access to the exception and the only way to guarantee cleanup on KeyboardInterrupt; ruff BLE001 exempts the same shape. Logging without re-raising, or wrapping in a new exception type, is still reported.\n\n[PY-COR-002 in the rule reference](https://github.com/AmitSinghOM/code-quality-analyzer/blob/v3.4.2/docs/RULES.md#py-cor-002-broad-exception-handler)",
                "text": "Catch the narrow exception types the operation can recover from."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AmitSinghOM/code-quality-analyzer/blob/v3.4.2/docs/RULES.md#py-cor-002-broad-exception-handler",
              "id": "PY-COR-002",
              "name": "broad-exception-handler",
              "properties": {
                "category": "correctness",
                "confidence": "high",
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "language": "python"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Broad exception handler"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "subprocess.*(cmd, shell=True), os.system(cmd) or os.popen(cmd) with a non-literal command lets a shell re-parse runtime data."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Shell command assembled at runtime** (`PY-SEC-002`)\n\nsubprocess.*(cmd, shell=True), os.system(cmd) or os.popen(cmd) with a non-literal command lets a shell re-parse runtime data.\n\n**CWE:** CWE-78\n\n**Remediation:** Pass an argument list without shell=True; quote with shlex.quote if a shell is unavoidable.\n\n**Not reported when:**\n\n- The command is a string literal, or the subprocess call has no shell=True keyword (a list of arguments is never reported).\n- os.system/os.popen are only recognised through the `os.` qualifier; a `from os import system` call is not seen.\n\n[PY-SEC-002 in the rule reference](https://github.com/AmitSinghOM/code-quality-analyzer/blob/v3.4.2/docs/RULES.md#py-sec-002-shell-command-assembled-at-runtime)",
                "text": "Pass an argument list without shell=True; quote with shlex.quote if a shell is unavoidable."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AmitSinghOM/code-quality-analyzer/blob/v3.4.2/docs/RULES.md#py-sec-002-shell-command-assembled-at-runtime",
              "id": "PY-SEC-002",
              "name": "shell-command-from-runtime-string",
              "properties": {
                "category": "security",
                "confidence": "medium",
                "defaultSeverity": "warning",
                "language": "python",
                "security-severity": "8.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "external/cwe/cwe-78"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Shell command assembled at runtime"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "eval() or exec() receives a value that is not a string literal."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**eval/exec of a runtime string** (`PY-SEC-003`)\n\neval() or exec() receives a value that is not a string literal.\n\n**CWE:** CWE-95\n\n**Remediation:** Use ast.literal_eval for data, a dispatch table for behaviour, or importlib for module names.\n\n**Not reported when:**\n\n- The first argument is a string literal, or the callee is an attribute (pandas `df.eval`, `ast.literal_eval`) rather than the bare builtin.\n\n[PY-SEC-003 in the rule reference](https://github.com/AmitSinghOM/code-quality-analyzer/blob/v3.4.2/docs/RULES.md#py-sec-003-evalexec-of-a-runtime-string)",
                "text": "Use ast.literal_eval for data, a dispatch table for behaviour, or importlib for module names."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/AmitSinghOM/code-quality-analyzer/blob/v3.4.2/docs/RULES.md#py-sec-003-evalexec-of-a-runtime-string",
              "id": "PY-SEC-003",
